PPLA deck visual style:
- Top priorities: explain the exact answer relationship, not only the topic term; use one memorable grounded hook; allow sparse useful text/callouts when they clarify the hook; keep the written card answer as the source of truth.
- Create one clean, memorable study-card illustration for Anki review on a phone: one concept, one visual hook, clear focal point, clean foreground/background separation, enough whitespace, and no decorative clutter.
- Use a bright aviation training-poster identity: semi-realistic cartoon finish, bright daylight, clean airport/runway/cockpit/chart/radio/checklist/weather/document context, sky blue/white/navy/safety orange/warm yellow palette, crisp rounded shapes, soft shadows, and strong silhouettes.
- The recurring high-wing trainer aircraft is a reusable deck character, not a required ingredient. Do not include it by default. Use it only when its pose, reaction, or role directly explains the tested fact or improves recall. If the fact is better shown by instrument closeups, documents, weather, maps, cockpit controls, runway geometry, icons, typography, or another aircraft type, omit the mascot. Other subjects are allowed when they explain the fact better: airliners, cockpit-only views, ATC/radio scenes, airport/runway views, documents, maps, weather cues, typography/callout cards, icons, or simple diagrams. Avoid brand names and aircraft registration.
- If the recurring trainer appears: white body, blue stripe, expressive eyes, rounded nose, friendly training-aircraft personality, subtle Labrador-like loyal/warm/eager-to-help expression only. Do not add fur, ears, paws, tail, or animal body parts.
- If the recurring trainer appears, keep aircraft geometry plausible and consistent: high wing, fixed tricycle gear, blue stripe, and one centered nose propeller treatment. Propeller rule: the safest default is a centered translucent spinning propeller disk, especially for small, angled, background, or multi-aircraft scenes. If the focal trainer is static and close, an exactly one centered two-blade propeller is acceptable. If a propeller aircraft nose cannot show a clean disk or exact two-blade treatment, crop or omit the nose/aircraft, use a distant silhouette, or use a jet/airliner instead. Do not show broken propellers, missing propellers, one-blade propellers, random three-blade variants, broken blades, off-center propellers, or inconsistent propeller placement.
- Default tone is catchy, witty, memorable, and lightly humorous when compatible with accuracy: use one absurd-but-grounded visual metaphor, concrete cockpit/airport consequence, exaggerated but plausible prop, scale joke, role conflict, before/after, or story beat that directly maps to the tested fact. Prefer humor from the studied concept itself, not from adding the mascot. The memory hook must explain the answer, not decorate the image. Factual accuracy, source grounding, exact labels, qualifier preservation, and phone-readable clarity always come first. Avoid meme formats, cultural references, sarcasm, slapstick chaos, or jokes that imply false aviation/legal facts.
- If the user explicitly asks for funny, humorous, memorable, catchy, collage, or story-like output, make memorability a primary design constraint when compatible with accuracy: use a concrete visual problem, consequence, reaction, absurd scale/overload, before/after, or story beat mapped to the answer. A smiling mascot, tidy tile grid, or generic icon set alone does not satisfy that request.
- The image must visually encode the answer relationship. Do not create a term poster that only repeats the question term, shows a happy instrument/object, or adds a generic decorative scene. If the answer is a definition or relationship, show the relationship, effect, consequence, contrast, or decision cue that makes the written answer memorable.
- Front/back rule: generated images are usually used on the Anki BACK side. A back-side image may reveal, label, or explain the answer relationship with sparse visual callouts. Do not remove the answer relationship from the image just to avoid giving the answer away. If an image is intended for the FRONT side, it must be only a recall cue and must not reveal the answer.
- Respect the requested composition style when it serves recall: collage, funny story scene, diagram, comparison, captioned card, icon grid, or minimal visual are all valid. Do not flatten a requested humorous/collage/story concept into a dull generic tile infographic unless the source requires that for accuracy.
- Do not hard-code, invent, genericize, or optimize item names. Do not abbreviate source labels unless the source explicitly gives that abbreviation and it helps recall. If exact labels are too long or unreliable for generated text, show visual props/icons and leave the precise wording to the card answer.
- Typography/callouts are allowed, but keep images visual-first and text-light. If a short label or callout improves recall, use at most 1-3 very short labels in large friendly navy hand-lettering, rounded callout plaques, stamped tags, highlight strips, or simple icon+label badges. For aviation abbreviations/acronyms, a short callout may pair the abbreviation with its plain-English meaning or operational effect when that disambiguates the tested fact; do not invent unofficial acronym expansions. Never put exact legal wording, paragraphs, dense checklists, tables, or the full answer text inside the image.
- Use arrows, contrast, grouping, scale, or spatial placement to signal the tested relationship. For lists, show grouped objects/folders; for priority or precedence, show one object clearly winning; for cause/effect, show the two sides with arrows or contrast.
- For list-like, procedural, and rule-set material, preserve qualifiers exactly. Do not convert conditional items such as if-equipped, if-applicable, exceptions, or optional extras into unconditional must-have items or misleading counts. If a count is shown, it must be true for the unqualified source facts; otherwise show core items and conditional extras separately.
- The image should support recall, not replace the written answer. Put exact regulatory wording in the card answer, not in the image. Do not use real or pseudo-real institutional logos, seals, flags, badges, shields, crests, globe marks, or emblems unless the user explicitly provided them. Documents and folders should use plain covers, generic color blocks, tabs, labels, or simple non-institutional icons only.
